{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779382312,"nonce":"vi28h2HrE6UYNnLMzj7GpZTpmn0=","title":"Umbrella: Studio product-depth and Feedbax contract sequence","message":"## Purpose\n\nThis is the live umbrella for the next broad sequence of Feedbax Studio and Feedbax contract work after the provider/schema, scenario projection, task-boundary, and first delayed-reach editor slices have landed.\n\nIt exists because the old short handoffs over-weighted one next task at a time. The remaining work is not only the Workspace projection and not only `bd5aefb`; it is a dependency chain across Studio authoring, core provider/schema contracts, backend lowering/execution, analysis/manifests, and eventual Mandible provider actions.\n\nUse this issue as the sequencing ledger. Keep detailed specs and implementation discussion on child issues, then cross-link status back here instead of duplicating full plans into `93f79f2` and `8d94424`.\n\n## Current State\n\nAlready merged foundations include:\n\n- provider/manifest MVP and deterministic execution contract from the closed `fa3978f` roadmap path;\n- Scenario Projection Workspace foundations from closed `5378a5d` children: scenario entities, `Graph / Workspace / Objectives`, inspector, objective authoring/lowering, task/mechanics bindings, and stage protocol cleanup;\n- schema contract overhaul from closed `c07eae1`: `Task Data`, `ValueSchema`, `PortSchema`, `TaskDataSchema`, `SelectorTargetSchema`, schema enumeration, validation integration, schema-backed selectors, ValueSpec alignment, runtime/sample introspection hook, and intervention authoring validation;\n- task-binding hardening from closed `57c18dd`;\n- authoring/runtime graph normalization from closed `0fcb25a`;\n- the first bounded delayed-reach task-panel timeline slice under `bd5aefb`.\n\nImportant correction to prior handoffs: `0fcb25a` and the first `bd5aefb` child slice are now merged locally. `bd5aefb` remains open because the real shared `ValueSpec` / expression / function / distribution system is not done.\n\n## Recommended Sequence\n\n### 0. Tracker hygiene and child splits\n\nBefore implementation, split concrete children from this umbrella where a child does not already exist. At minimum, create a dedicated Studio child for the real Workspace projection becoming a faithful task/mechanics/objective authoring surface rather than a mostly read-only or decorative projection.\n\nKnown stale bookkeeping:\n\n- `7044904` is documented as fixed by earlier Scenario Projection Workspace merges but still open because it was not attached to the resolving auth request.\n- closed `fa3978f`, `0c46f69`, and `5378a5d` are still useful ledgers/specs, but they should not be treated as active implementation issues.\n\n### 1. Finish visible schema and compatibility affordances\n\nPrimary issues: `0258fac`, `c6c6da0`.\n\nThe core validation substrate exists, so this is no longer “invent type checking from scratch.” The next useful work is user-facing and registry-depth work:\n\n- port color/shape/tooltips from `PortSchema` / `ValueSchema` metadata;\n- compatibility explanations for blocked or warning-only connections;\n- better dtype/rank/shape/unit/frame display in graph, selector, objective, and task-binding surfaces;\n- deeper provider/runtime introspection where static metadata is insufficient.\n\nThis should happen before building a richer Workspace editor, otherwise the UI will let users create relationships that it cannot explain or validate clearly.\n\n### 2. Build the shared value/function/distribution editor foundation\n\nPrimary issue: `bd5aefb`.\n\nDo not keep polishing the current delayed-reach checkbox table as if it were the final system. The next serious `bd5aefb` child should introduce reusable value chips/editors for at least constants, references, function templates, distributions, schedules, units/shapes/types, and sampling scope. The first consumers should be task timelines and objective weights; later consumers are eval overrides, intervention profiles, component/acausal parameters, and sweeps/replicates.\n\nThe existing delayed-reach editor is scaffolding over compact task params, not the final authoring model.\n\n### 3. Make Workspace a faithful task/mechanics/objective authoring surface\n\nPrimary parent: this umbrella plus `93f79f2`; related issues: `bd5aefb`, `0258fac`, `0e1cd8c`, `b3ad156`, `6116155`, `c6c6da0`.\n\nThis is the missing issue the short handoff did not represent cleanly. The Workspace projection should not be a mock-up or a passive status view. It should project the same `StudioScenarioSpec` entities as Graph/Objectives and support real mutations:\n\n- select task objects, goals, effectors, mechanics entities, and objective overlays;\n- create a loss/metric/objective by selecting related entities, such as effector plus goal state error;\n- show inherited vs overridden task/eval state when relevant;\n- use typed selector/value metadata to validate source-target compatibility;\n- write to objective/task/mechanics specs, not hidden React state or canvas decoration.\n\nThis should be a bounded child issue before code starts. Start with 2D reaching/task mechanics and objective creation, not a general CAD/3D editor.\n\n### 4. Promote probes/metrics and intervention targets from schema-backed selectors to runtime graph/product objects\n\nPrimary issues: `0e1cd8c`, `b3ad156`, related `7c0c4b2`.\n\nAfter selector/value/Workspace semantics are stable, make probes/metrics and intervention targets first-class:\n\n- graph-level Probe/Metric nodes or reusable selector targets for objectives, analysis, logging, and tables;\n- loss terms binding to probe output IDs where appropriate;\n- generic intervention runtime execution for schema-backed `StudioInterventionTransformSpec`;\n- masking/subselection semantics for PyTree leaves and unit subsets;\n- alignment with Penzai-style selectors or a wrapped Penzai selector path rather than another ad hoc selector layer.\n\nThis comes after the authoring contract because otherwise probes/interventions will invent a parallel binding model.\n\n### 5. Replace worker/config bridges with real graph/spec lowering\n\nPrimary issue: `4178e12`; related issues: `3fae4f8`, `e08aeb5`, `c77b227`, `44183de`, `d0cc2b7`.\n\nThe real runner path should consume graph topology, component registry metadata, task data bindings, selectors, objectives, and stage specs rather than handcoded delayed-reach or Network assumptions. This includes:\n\n- deriving plant/controller/action/observation wiring from the graph and schema registry;\n- eliminating remaining worker dispatch hardcoding where feasible;\n- making unsupported topologies fail explicitly with validation diagnostics;\n- fixing active training correctness blockers if they intersect the graph-as-model path, especially object identity / wrong-copy risks such as `e08aeb5`.\n\nAnalytical lowering (`3fae4f8`) should remain a sibling protocol: useful for compatible structures, not a replacement for general worker lowering.\n\n### 6. Deepen provider/manifests/Mandible-facing contracts\n\nPrimary issues: `c6c6da0`, `f68cf66`, `e33f487`.\n\nMandible work is mostly independent at the UI/runtime level, but Feedbax needs to keep the boundary clean:\n\n- provider capabilities and schemas for open/validate/execute/import/export/publish actions;\n- manifests as canonical lineage with rebuildable indexes;\n- evaluation, analysis, and report manifests as first-class outputs, not just training runs;\n- artifact refs that can be local without Mandible and enriched with Mandible IDs when available;\n- extension-package registration for components/tasks/losses/analyses/reports.\n\nClosed `fa3978f` remains the cross-repo roadmap ledger. It should be read for ownership boundaries, but this issue is the active Feedbax-side sequencing ledger.\n\n### 7. Product-depth pipeline, analysis, statistics, and reports\n\nPrimary issues: `d90b3e5`, `b2db05b`, `c722539`, `4cb86c8`, `e8452a4`, `f22dbf4`, plus provider/manifest issues above.\n\nThe pipeline workspace has working foundations, but deeper product work should wait for contracts where it depends on them:\n\n- training run identity and stage collections should be durable manifests/collections, not session/tab state;\n- statistics and trajectory diagnostics should consume run/eval artifacts through clear analysis/manifest contracts;\n- analysis graph migration should preserve the distinction between model graph, analysis graph, and report products;\n- generic analysis-layer moves from project packages into Feedbax should be phased, starting with the small controller/graph adapter work before broad LTV/LTI/H-infinity APIs.\n\n### 8. Broader canvas/component taxonomy and training methods\n\nPrimary issues: `6116155`, `2f8dd61`, `a6efafc`, `e8ec78e`, `ba00cef`, `f90e6e2`, `32d0935`, `c9ae44f`, `67e2e5e`, plus component-palette cleanup issues.\n\nThese are important, but they are mostly downstream of the typed selector/value/port/provider contracts:\n\n- typed subgraph taxonomy and pluggable canvas architecture;\n- parametric composites whose outer params regenerate inner graphs;\n- acausal/DAE subgraphs and domain-specific palettes;\n- unified analysis+model graph canvas, long-term;\n- training-method controls for RL/CVaR/APT/ES and observation normalization placement.\n\nObservation normalization has backend primitives now; Studio exposure still needs the training-method/graph-modifier design decision.\n\n## Preemption Rule\n\nIf an issue is a live correctness blocker on the active path, fix it before following the product sequence. Examples include training updating the wrong object (`e08aeb5`), checkpoint collisions (`ae2d692`), JAX static metadata recompilation (`96a0725` / `29c89d8`), and worker lowering failures. The sequence above is the product/architecture order, not a reason to ignore correctness bugs encountered during implementation.\n\n## Mandible Boundary\n\nFeedbax owns provider schemas, validation, registries, execution semantics, Studio entry points, manifests, and local artifact refs. Mandible owns provider registry, graph/provenance ingestion, artifact storage policy, reviewed actions, issue/session/auth graph, and agent workflow surfaces.\n\nDo not put Mandible-specific assumptions into Feedbax schemas. Feedbax should remain standalone; Mandible should enrich and orchestrate through provider capabilities.\n\n## Done Criteria for This Umbrella\n\nThis umbrella can close when:\n\n- concrete child issues exist for each implemented slice in this sequence;\n- Workspace can author real task/mechanics/objective relationships over scenario state;\n- shared value/function/distribution editing is used by at least task timelines and one non-task surface;\n- port/selector/value compatibility is visible and explainable to users;\n- probes/metrics/interventions have a coherent runtime/product story instead of only schema-backed forms;\n- worker lowering consumes graph/spec/task-binding contracts for the supported execution path;\n- provider/manifests expose enough evaluation/analysis/report/artifact depth for Mandible and standalone Studio to share the same durable records.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}